+
+`list` orders jobs as active, then unverified, then finished, each group by
+creation time. `list --sort created|finished` sets an explicit order; it accepts
+only those two values and works with `--all`, `--json` and `--resources`. It
+changes the order only, never which jobs are shown, and human and JSON output use
+the same order. With either value, active jobs come first, then unverified jobs
+(state `unknown`, or a finished state with no recorded finish time); both groups
+are ordered by creation time, oldest first. Jobs with a recorded finish time
+follow: oldest created first for `created`, most recently finished first for
+`finished`. Ties on the sort time are broken by job ID, ascending. Like `list`
+itself, sorting never renews a lease. `watch` has no `--sort` option.
 Use a filesystem that preserves atomic file replacement for the state directory.
 VM shared folders can violate that requirement; use native Linux storage when
 running inside a Linux VM or container.
